.TH picoctl 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
picoctl \- control the machine itself
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B picoctl flash
.br
.B picoctl keymap
.I country
.br
.B picoctl mode
[\fInumber\fR]
.br
.B picoctl usbreset
.br
.B picoctl numlock
[\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR] [\fIvid\fB:\fIpid\fR] [\fB\-\-once\fR|\fB\-\-load\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B picoctl
reaches the parts of the Pico Computer that are not files: the
bootloader, the keyboard, the display mode and the USB host.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B flash
Reboot into the UF2 bootloader, so a new kernel can be copied on.
.I Remount the root read-only first.
This is an immediate reset: the kernel unmounts nothing on the way out,
so a root left read-write comes back dirty and the next boot spends
minutes in
.BR fsck .
.TP
.BI keymap " country"
Set the keyboard layout: one of
.BR us ,
.BR uk ,
.BR de ,
.BR fr ,
.BR es ,
.BR be .
.TP
.BI mode " number"
Set the display mode.  With no number, return to the 80x40 text
console.
.TP
.B usbreset
Re-enumerate the USB bus, after moving the DPDT switch between
programming and hub.
.TP
.B numlock
Report the num lock setting.
.B numlock on
or
.B numlock off
sets it for the keyboard plugged in now, and remembers it against that
keyboard.  A
.IB vid : pid
sets it for a keyboard that is not plugged in.
.B \-\-once
changes it without saving.
.B \-\-load
replays the saved settings, which is what
.B /etc/rc
does at boot.
.SH WHY NUM LOCK IS REMEMBERED PER KEYBOARD
Whether a keyboard has a numeric keypad is not discoverable from its USB
descriptors, so there is nothing to detect.  The setting is remembered
against the keyboard identity instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# remount -n / ro
# picoctl flash
# picoctl keymap uk
# picoctl mode 2
# picoctl mode
# picoctl numlock off
# picoctl numlock on 04b3:3025
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR remount (1),
.BR reboot (1),
.BR stty (1)
